  (text "Commander battery module (rev B0) - power path.\n\nSix standard AAA NiMH cells (rechargeable, user-replaceable, sold everywhere; 6S = 7.2 V nominal, 6.0 - 9.4 V) in\nthe tray of the chassis model (Group#56: 3 columns x 2 cells, x 57-93, y 77-166).  The cells sit in a plastic\nholder with spring contacts; the holder's harness plugs into J1 (B+ twice, B- twice: 2 A per PH contact).\n\ncells -> PTC F1 -> P-MOSFET Q1 (reverse polarity: a cell put in the wrong way round cannot hurt anything)\n-> VBAT -> TPS563201 buck -> 5.0 V on the USB-C port (VBUS) -> mainboard battery port J40\nVBUS (5 V from the mainboard, when it charges the module) -> MT3608 boost -> D1 -> VBAT -> cells\n\nThe buck is ON by default (EN divider = hardware under-voltage cut-off at about 5.8 V = 0.97 V per cell); the\nmicrocontroller can switch it off (Q2).  It runs in Eco-mode, so it never sinks current: while the mainboard\npushes 5.05-5.1 V into the port to charge the module, the buck (set to 4.98 V) simply idles, and it takes over\nby itself the moment that voltage goes away - a UPS without a switch-over.  See README.md, 'How it works'.\n\nCharging is constant-current, controlled by the microcontroller: it measures the cell current (shunt R2 + INA181)\nand trims the boost's set point by a filtered PWM injected into its feedback node.  Hardware ceiling with the PWM\npin low: 9.4 V (1.56 V per cell); with the pin released (microcontroller off or reset) the boost sits at 4 V,\nbelow the cells, and EN is pulled low - it cannot charge without the microcontroller." (exclude_from_sim no) (at 15.24 25.4 0)
    (effects (font (size 1.524 1.524)) (justify left top)) (uuid "b7d1483b-8032-5b1e-a85b-93d9c48ac563"))
